{
  "claims_summary": "The document introduces the Agentic Military AI Governance Framework (AMAGF) to address specific control failures inherent in modern agentic AI systems used in military operations. It identifies six distinct failure modes—ranging from interpretive divergence to cascade severance—that traditional automation governance fails to mitigate. By shifting the conception of human control from a binary state to a continuous, measurable 'Control Quality Score' (CQS), the framework provides a mechanism for real-time monitoring and graduated responses to control degradation. The AMAGF emphasizes institutional accountability, assigning specific responsibilities for preventive, detective, and corrective governance to developers, commanders, and regulators to ensure meaningful human control throughout the operational lifecycle.",
  "i_nodes": [
    {
      "id": "D-1",
      "text": "Agentic AI systems introduce distinct control failures not addressed by existing safety frameworks.",
      "type": "empirical",
      "taxonomy_refs": ["saf-beliefs-042", "saf-intentions-088"],
      "policy_refs": ["pol-093"]
    },
    {
      "id": "D-2",
      "text": "Governance must move from a binary conception of control to a continuous model in which control quality is actively measured and managed.",
