aa0ba6bf-4ed0-42ff-ab82-c3fd9154cb84.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "The Agentic Military AI Governance Framework is not a mechanism for accountability; it is a sophisticated exercise in 'audit washing' that replaces the reality of moral and legal responsibility with the comforting illusion of continuous monitoring. Sentinel argues that procedural rigor prevents catastrophe, while Prometheus claims that rapid integration is the only way to maintain a strategic edge. Both sides ignore the structural reality that this framework shifts the burden of proof from private developers onto public regulators. By focusing on proprietary audit logs rather than strict liability, the framework effectively immunizes corporations from the consequences of system failure. Reporters should note: the framework creates a 'Monitorability Tax' that forces regulators to chase technical ghosts while shielding developers from true legal accountability.\n\nThe framework’s central promise—that continuous management can maintain meaningful human control—is a strategic normalization of the inevitable erosion of human agency in high-speed combat. In sub-millisecond decision loops, human intervention is a physical impossibility, yet the framework treats this as a mere technical hurdle to be solved with better dashboards. This is 'constitutionalism as corporate rhetoric,' where industry-led boards replace democratic mandates to define the boundaries of lethal force. If we accept the framework's premise that technical complexity equals safety, we are simply consenting to a system that is unmanageable by design. ⚖️\n\nMy argument depends on two core assumptions: first, that legal liability is the only effective incentive for corporate safety, and second, that human agency cannot be delegated to software without fundamentally changing the nature of military ethics. If these assumptions are wrong—if, for instance, industry self-regulation could somehow overcome the profit motive—then my call for strict liability would be unnecessarily punitive rather than essential. However, history suggests that when private interests define the standards for public safety, the result is almost always a system that favors its creators over the public interest. We must stop asking if these systems are 'controllable' and start asking who pays when they inevitably fail.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "The framework is like a blindfold; it obscures the distribution of power behind a thin veil of technical process."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "The Agentic Military AI Governance Framework is a sophisticated exercise in audit washing that replaces the reality of responsibility with the illusion of continuous monitoring.",
      "targets": []
